<article class="env-lemma" id="0BDV">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BDV">Lemma <span data-tag="0BDV">12.24.8</span>.</a> In the situation of Definition <a href="/tag/0BDU" data-tag="0BDU">12.24.7</a>. Let $Z_ r^{p, q}, B_ r^{p, q} \subset E_{r_0}^{p, q}$ be the $(p, q)$-graded parts of $Z_ r, B_ r$ defined as in Section <a href="/tag/011M" data-tag="011M">12.20</a>. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
The spectral sequence is regular if and only if for all $p, q$ there exists an $r = r(p, q)$ such that $Z_ r^{p, q} = Z_{r + 1}^{p, q} = \ldots $ 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The spectral sequence is coregular if and only if for all $p, q$ there exists an $r = r(p, q)$ such that $B_ r^{p, q} = B_{r + 1}^{p, q} = \ldots $ 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The spectral sequence is bounded if and only if it is both bounded below and bounded above. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If the spectral sequence is bounded below, then it is regular. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If the spectral sequence is bounded above, then it is coregular. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Omitted. Hint: If $E_ r^{p, q} = 0$, then we have $E_{r'}^{p, q} = 0$ for all $r' \geq r$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>